From b23849aa44c96aeecb0bf864185718091f3f7047 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 11:59:21 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] drm: Show a frame sequential commit's eye for the refresh it
 reaches

KWin's software frame sequential twins choose a commit's eye for the
refresh its frame targets. A commit that reaches the commit thread
late, or that the thread submits late, lands on a later refresh, and
when that refresh is an odd number later it showed the wrong eye. On
the VKMS bench such late commits, not late page-flip events, are where
most of the wrong-eye slots come from.

The commit now carries, with the eye chosen for its frame, the refresh
it was chosen for and the primary plane's source x for either eye. Just
before the commit thread submits it, the eye is set for the refresh
the submission reaches, the first one whose vblank starts after now,
counted in refreshes from the one it was chosen for: an odd number
later shows the other eye. The eye reported to the pipeline when the
flip completes is the one committed. Held pairs are re-committed the
same way, so their eye order follows the refreshes they land on.
Commits without a frame keep their eye, and nothing changes for other
layouts, 2D included, or for DisplayPort's frame sequential stereo,
where the display engine alternates the eyes.

testMockDrmFrameSequential::testLateCommitShowsItsRefreshEye presents a
frame for refresh 101, a right eye's, after refresh 101 has begun; the
commit must reach the kernel with refresh 102's left eye.
testDrmCommitThread::testRetargetedEyeIsTheCommittedOne covers the
counting and that the reported eye is the committed one.

